{
  "id": "remove-duplicates-from-sorted-array",
  "questionId": "remove-duplicates-from-sorted-array",
  "slug": "remove-duplicates-from-sorted-array",
  "title": "Remove Duplicates from Sorted Array",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Two pointers",
  "patterns": [
    "two pointers",
    "in-place array"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "since the array is sorted, any duplicate of a value sits right next to it. do you actually need a set to know if you've seen a value before?",
    "what if one pointer tracked 'the last unique value I've kept' and another scanned forward looking for something different?",
    "keep a slow pointer at the last written unique value; move a fast pointer through the array, and whenever it finds a value different from the slow pointer's, write it and advance slow",
    "by the end, everything up to and including the slow pointer is your deduplicated result"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "using a hash set anyway, missing the point of the sorted-array shortcut",
    "comparing the fast pointer to the wrong thing (e.g. the previous fast position instead of the last kept value)",
    "off-by-one when building the final result from the slow pointer's position"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a list of numbers sorted in ascending order, possibly with\nrepeats.\n\n**Return the list of unique values, in the same order, with every duplicate removed.**\n\nDo it by scanning the array once, without sorting it again or using extra data structures to track what you've seen.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= nums.length <= 3 * 10^4",
      "-100 <= nums[i] <= 100",
      "nums is sorted in non-decreasing order"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "every element is the same",
      "no duplicates at all",
      "a single element"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
